body:
Consider this code where I parse a PromQL query and try to get the name of time series being queried:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"

	"github.com/prometheus/prometheus/model/labels"
	"github.com/prometheus/prometheus/promql/parser"
)

func parseNode(node parser.Node) {
	switch n := node.(type) {
	case *parser.VectorSelector:
		for _, lm := range n.LabelMatchers {
			if lm.Name == labels.MetricName {
				fmt.Printf("Name: %s\n", lm.Value)
			}
		}
	}
}

func main() {
	expr, err := parser.ParseExpr("foo{}")
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Printf("Error: %s\n", err)
	}
	parseNode(expr)
}
```

With Prometheus 3.8 this now throws a deprecation warning:

```
labels.MetricName is deprecated: Use schema.Metadata structure and its methods.
```

But looking at https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/prometheus/prometheus@v0.308.0/schema#pkg-index I don't see any way to get the time series name to compare it against matchers. It's all focused on time series results labels.
Can we either export MetricName from schema package or un-deprecate it in labels package?

### Problem Space

Common complain when we logic around samples in Prometheus codebase is the fact we have so many different types and semi-repeated code with float vs (integer) histogram vs float histogram. Common examples:

* [Repeated switches like in appender V2 ](https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/blob/0d116b09944cf98822b7434ef5a132640ff437cf/tsdb/head_append_v2.go#L152)

```
switch {
	case fh != nil:
		isStale = value.IsStaleNaN(fh.Sum)
		appErr = a.appendFloatHistogram(s, t, fh, opts.RejectOutOfOrder)
	case h != nil:
		isStale = value.IsStaleNaN(h.Sum)
		appErr = a.appendHistogram(s, t, h, opts.RejectOutOfOrder)
	default:
		isStale = value.IsStaleNaN(v)
		if isStale {
```

* [Verbose function calls with arbitrary logic of telling what's provided](https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/blob/0d116b09944cf98822b7434ef5a132640ff437cf/storage/interface_append.go#L168) 

```
// v, h, fh represents sample value for each sample type.
	// Callers MUST only provide one of the sample types (either v, h or fh).
	// Implementations can detect the type of the sample with the following switch:
	//
	// switch {
	//  case fh != nil: It's a float histogram append.
	//  case h != nil: It's a histogram append.
	//  default: It's a float append.
	// }
	// TODO(bwplotka): We plan to experiment on using generics for complex sampleType, but do it after we unify interface (derisk) and before we add native summaries.
	// ...
	Append(ref SeriesRef, ls labels.Labels, st, t int64, v float64, h *histogram.Histogram, fh *histogram.FloatHistogram, opts AppendV2Options) (SeriesRef, error)
```

(TODO: Any other good examples of the issues I missed? cc @bboreham @beorn7 @krajorama ?)

Solving this would make Prometheus easier to maintain, extend. It might also be more reliable (e.g. accidental Append of `0, nil, nil` could mean both "error" or 0 float sample append).  

Notably it unblocks ideas like:
* [Native Summaries](https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/issues/16949)
* [Native Stateset](https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/issues/17914)

### Acceptance Criteria

* We have a mechanism for robust way of generic logic per sample "type".

So e.g. appender could look like:

```
Append(ref SeriesRef, ls labels.Labels, st, t int64, v sample.Value, opts AppendV2Options) (SeriesRef, error)
```

* Adding another sample type is easier.
* Hot path of float sample is not significantly slower/alloc more memory 

### Ideas

Generics is an obvious choice. But in the past they incur an overhead on function calls. We might double check if it's now a bit more optimized or/and if the overhead is now acceptable. Having everywhere ` float, compositeType` is also an option.

This problem could be equally solved with interfaces too.

There are also some fun projects that help with union-like logic: https://github.com/widmogrod/mkunion

## Bug Description

In `tsdb/head.go`, the `deleteSeriesByID` function accesses `hashes[hashShard]` while only holding `locks[refShard]`:

```go
func (h *Head) deleteSeriesByID(refs []chunks.HeadSeriesRef) {
    // ...
    for _, ref := range refs {
        refShard := int(ref) & (h.series.size - 1)
        h.series.locks[refShard].Lock()

        // ...

        hash := series.lset.Hash()
        hashShard := int(hash) & (h.series.size - 1)

        // ...

        h.series.hashes[hashShard].del(hash, series.ref)  // BUG: accessing hashes[hashShard] without holding locks[hashShard]
        delete(h.series.series[refShard], series.ref)

        h.series.locks[refShard].Unlock()
    }
}
```

https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/blob/main/tsdb/head.go#L2159-L2189

The `hashes` map is protected by `locks[hashShard]`, not `locks[refShard]`. When `hashShard != refShard`, this is a data race.

## Comparison with Correct Pattern

The `gc()` function in the same file correctly acquires both locks when they differ:

```go
// From gc() check function:
refShard := int(series.ref) & (s.size - 1)
if hashShard != refShard {
    s.locks[refShard].Lock()
    defer s.locks[refShard].Unlock()
}
// Now safe to access both hashes[hashShard] and series[refShard]
```

https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/blob/main/tsdb/head.go#L2081-L2085

## Current Impact

The function is documented as "Only used for WAL replay", and during WAL replay the Head typically isn't concurrently accessed, so this may not cause issues in practice currently. However:

1. It violates the locking contract established elsewhere in the codebase
2. It could cause data races if the function is used in other contexts or if WAL replay behavior changes
3. Race detectors may flag this

## Suggested Fix

Acquire `locks[hashShard]` when it differs from `refShard`, similar to the pattern in `gc()`:

```go
refShard := int(ref) & (h.series.size - 1)
h.series.locks[refShard].Lock()

// ... get series, compute hash ...

hashShard := int(hash) & (h.series.size - 1)
if hashShard != refShard {
    h.series.locks[hashShard].Lock()
}

// ... do deletion ...

if hashShard != refShard {
    h.series.locks[hashShard].Unlock()
}
h.series.locks[refShard].Unlock()
```

## Summary

When a label name or value exceeds 16MB (2^24 bytes), the `sizeWhenEncoded()` function panics with a plain string instead of an error. The parser's `recover()` function then attempts to cast this string to an `error` type, causing a second panic with a type assertion error.

## Expected Behavior

The parser should return a proper error when a label exceeds the maximum allowed size, not panic.


## Root Cause

### 1. `[sizeWhenEncoded()](https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/blob/main/model/labels/labels_stringlabels.go#L543-L550)` panics with a string (not an error)


```go
func sizeWhenEncoded(x uint64) (n int) {
	if x < 255 {
		return 1
	} else if x <= 1<<24 {
		return 4
	}
	panic("String too long to encode as label.")  // <-- panics with a string, not an error
}
```

### 2. `[recover()](https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/blob/main/promql/parser/parse.go#L340-L354)` assumes panic value is always an `error`

```go
// recover is the handler that turns panics into returns from the top level of Parse.
func (*parser) recover(errp *error) {
	e := recover()
	switch _, ok := e.(runtime.Error); {
	case ok:
		// Print the stack trace but do not inhibit the running application.
		buf := make([]byte, 64<<10)
		buf = buf[:runtime.Stack(buf, false)]

		fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "parser panic: %v\n%s", e, buf)
		*errp = errUnexpected
	case e != nil:
		*errp = e.(error)  // <-- type assertion fails because e is a string, not an error
	}
}
```

## Stack Trace

```
github.com/prometheus/prometheus/model/labels.labelsSize(...)
    /vendor/github.com/prometheus/prometheus/model/labels/labels_stringlabels.go:569
github.com/prometheus/prometheus/model/labels.labelSize(...)
    /vendor/github.com/prometheus/prometheus/model/labels/labels_stringlabels.go:579
github.com/prometheus/prometheus/model/labels.sizeWhenEncoded(...)
    /usr/local/go/src/runtime/panic.go:783 +0x120
panic({0x3fbdd80?, 0x5eb93a0?})
    /vendor/github.com/prometheus/prometheus/promql/parser/parse.go:339 +0x170
github.com/prometheus/prometheus/promql/parser.(*parser).recover(0x40031041c0?, 0x40257c17b0)
    /usr/local/go/src/runtime/panic.go:783 +0x120
panic({0x42949e0?, 0x4020f56240?})
    ...
panic: interface conversion: string is not error: missing method Error
```

## Suggested Fix

### Change `sizeWhenEncoded()` to panic with an error

```go
func sizeWhenEncoded(x uint64) (n int) {
	if x < 255 {
		return 1
	} else if x <= 1<<24 {
		return 4
	}
	panic(errors.New("string too long to encode as label"))
}
```

Alongside this we should alse make `parser.recover` more robust and make it handle non-error panics gracefully.



## Reproduction

Create a label with a value exceeding 16MB (2^24 = 16,777,216 bytes):

```go
func TestLabelTooLong(t *testing.T) {
    const maxLabelSize = 1 << 24 // 16MB
    longValue := make([]byte, maxLabelSize+1)
    for i := range longValue {
        longValue[i] = 'a'
    }
    input := `{job="` + string(longValue) + `"}`

    // This will panic instead of returning an error
    _, err := parser.ParseMetric(input)
    // Expected: err != nil
    // Actual: panic
}
```

I was inspired to look into this while reviewing the OTel -> NHCB specification in https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-specification/pull/5091. 

### Background

When sending a histogram with classic buckets over PRW 2.0 today, you currently need to send them as either NHCB (by setting schema to -53, and storing bucket boundaries using native histogram fields), or as separate series. This is aligned with the way histograms are stored in the TSDB today, but is different from how scrape formats handle histograms. PRW 2.0 is the only transport format today which uses NHCB. Prometheus protobuf [uses separate buckets](https://github.com/prometheus/client_model/blob/2d24e7e91dcab27ace797932a2a21081cb959502/io/prometheus/client/metrics.proto#L116), and OpenMetrics 2.0 also [uses separate buckets](https://prometheus.io/docs/specs/om/open_metrics_spec_2_0/#histogram-with-classic-buckets).

We could consider adding a buckets field to the PRW 2.0 histogram, and deprecating the existing field:

```proto
message Histogram {
  // ...
  // custom_values is deprecated and no longer used in this version of the protocol.
  // Use classic_buckets for classic histograms.
  repeated double custom_values = 16 [deprecated = true];
  // ...
  // Classic buckets.
  // This allows sending histograms with explicit bucket boundaries and cumulative counts.
  // If both native buckets (exponential) and classic buckets are present,
  // it represents a combined histogram.
  repeated ClassicBucket classic_buckets = 18;
}

message ClassicBucket {
  double upper_bound = 1;
  double cumulative_count = 2;
}
```

Pros:

* Return NHCB to be an internal implementation detail of the TSDB (is this even feasible?), and keep PRW consistent with other protocols.
* Support sending "combined" Native histogram + Classic histogram for compatibility during migrations.
    * Caveat: The prometheus server won't be able to send both without changes to its storage.

Drawbacks:

* Migration from `custom_values` to `classic_buckets`.

Requirements:

* Prometheus continues to support receiving NHCB.

Discussed this briefly with @bwplotka and @krajorama. We are skeptical that it is worth it.

A global feature flag for PromQL ST processing means that all queries will use ST. Given that we know there is bad instrumentation out there sending bad start times already, this may cause errors in query results that would essentially be breaking users who are otherwise trying to test out delta support with known-good instrumentation. Therefore we need a per-query way to override the default ST processing behavior to help users debug / test their installations. 

We currently think that an HTTP header that overrides the configuration flag is the way to go. Users can use the header before toggling the flag to test what their queries will look like in a non-destructive  way. And then after toggling the flag, they can use the opposite header value to revert the behavior to see if ST processing is the issue.

