## Problem
While browsing through the TSDB append logic, I noticed a `TODO` left by `@codesome` in `tsdb/head_append.go` suggesting that we refactor `Commit()` to make it more readable:

https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/blob/main/tsdb/head_append.go#L1717

```go
// Commit writes to the WAL and adds the data to the Head.
// TODO(codesome): Refactor this method to reduce indentation and make it more readable.
func (a *headAppenderBase) Commit() (err error) {
```

I'd like to help clean this up as a good first issue!

I plan to refactor the Commit() function by pulling nested blocks into smaller helper functions or by using early returns where applicable to flatten the indentation and improve readability, as requested in the TODO.

### What did you do?

I benchmarked `AppenderV2` appends to the TSDB head with `EnableSTAsZeroSample` (`created-timestamp-zero-ingestion`), for long-running counters. Their start timestamps are older than the head's min valid time.

### What did you expect to see?

About the same cost as without `EnableSTAsZeroSample`. A start timestamp that old gets no zero sample, which is the normal case for a cumulative counter.

### What did you see instead?

Three extra heap allocations per sample. `headAppenderV2.Append` calls [`bestEffortAppendSTZeroSample`](https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/blob/79cee810837a231d0f571623e5636ad6854d0260/tsdb/head_append_v2.go#L397-L445) for every sample with a non-zero start timestamp. For these counters it returns here:

```go
if st < a.minValidTime {
	a.head.logger.Debug("Error when appending ST", "series", ls.String(), "st", st, "t", t, "err", storage.ErrOutOfBounds)
	return s
}
```

The arguments are evaluated whether or not debug logging is enabled. `ls.String()` builds the label string, and the variadic `[]any` and the boxed timestamp go to the heap. The `st >= t` check and the error path at the end log the same way.

The benchmark below does 1000 appends per op without a WAL. On an Apple M4 Pro:

| | Time per op | Bytes per op | Allocations per op |
|---|---|---|---|
| `EnableSTAsZeroSample=false` | 170 µs | 5.9 KiB | 47 |
| `EnableSTAsZeroSample=true` | 414 µs | 108 KiB | 3047 |

Guarding the `st < a.minValidTime` log with `a.head.logger.Enabled(ctx, slog.LevelDebug)` brought allocations back to the `false` level. In a variant of the benchmark with a WAL, the time overhead was about 13%, with the same 3000 extra allocations.

Since an old start timestamp is expected for cumulative counters, should this case log at all? The other two logs could be guarded with `Enabled`. Also, the comment at the top of the function says to use `ls` instead of `s.lset` to avoid locking the series, but the error path logs `s.lset.String()`.

```go
// One op appends a sample to each of 1000 long-running counters, whose start timestamp is older than the
// head's min valid time.
func BenchmarkAppendWithOldST(b *testing.B) {
	for _, zeroIngestion := range []bool{false, true} {
		b.Run(fmt.Sprintf("EnableSTAsZeroSample=%v", zeroIngestion), func(b *testing.B) {
			opts := DefaultHeadOptions()
			opts.ChunkDirRoot = b.TempDir()
			opts.EnableSTAsZeroSample = zeroIngestion
			h, err := NewHead(nil, nil, nil, nil, opts, nil)
			require.NoError(b, err)
			defer h.Close()

			const st = 1
			t0 := 10 * DefaultBlockDuration
			lbls := make([]labels.Labels, 1000)
			for i := range lbls {
				lbls[i] = labels.FromStrings("__name__", "http_requests_total", "job", "api", "instance", "10.0.0.1:9090", "idx", strconv.Itoa(i))
			}
			append := func(ts int64) {
				app := h.AppenderV2(b.Context())
				for _, l := range lbls {
					_, err := app.Append(0, l, st, ts, 1, nil, nil, storage.AOptions{})
					require.NoError(b, err)
				}
				require.NoError(b, app.Commit())
			}
			append(t0) // Moves the head's min valid time past st.

			b.ReportAllocs()
			b.ResetTimer()
			for n := 1; n <= b.N; n++ {
				append(t0 + int64(n)*15_000)
			}
		})
	}
}
```

### Prometheus version

`main` at 79cee8108.

### What did you do?

Configured `otlp.keep_identifying_resource_attributes: true` and ingested OTLP metrics whose resource only contains identifying resource attributes (`service.name`, `service.namespace`, and/or `service.instance.id`) and no other resource attributes.

### What did you expect to see?

A `target_info` series is emitted containing `job`, `instance`, and the identifying resource attribute labels (`service_name`, `service_namespace`, `service_instance_id`).

### What did you see instead? Under which circumstances?

No `target_info` series is emitted. In [`addResourceTargetInfo`](https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/blob/ed2af99b8b6609b35e7e8ff3ba5a1e606e920321/storage/remote/otlptranslator/prometheusremotewrite/helper.go#L565-L591), `nonIdentifyingAttrsCount == 0` returns early before checking `settings.KeepIdentifyingResourceAttributes`:

```go
	nonIdentifyingAttrsCount := attributes.Len()
	for _, a := range identifyingAttrs {
		_, haveAttr := attributes.Get(a)
		if haveAttr {
			nonIdentifyingAttrsCount--
		}
	}
	if nonIdentifyingAttrsCount == 0 {
		// If we only have job + instance, then target_info isn't useful, so don't add it.
		return nil
	}
```

When `keep_identifying_resource_attributes: true`, `target_info` carries `service_name`, `service_namespace`, and `service_instance_id` in addition to `job` and `instance`, so it should still be emitted when at least one identifying attribute is present.

